  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 135 AM CDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nDense fog is ongoing early this morning across much of the\nNorthland, leading to drastically reduced visibilities for the \nmorning commute. A Dense Fog Advisory is in effect until mid-\nmorning to highlight this travel hazard. Once the fog lifts \nlater this morning, we will see a seasonably very warm day \nacross the region. Afternoon highs are expected to approach the \nupper 70s for pockets of northwest Wisconsin. Later this evening\nand through the overnight hours, an incoming band of heavy \nrainfall will setup over north-central Minnesota. The heaviest \nprecipitation is expected to fall mainly over northwest Itasca \nCounty and western Koochiching County where upwards of an inch \nis possible. A few rumbles of thunder cannot be ruled out either\ntonight for these areas.\n\nHeading into Tuesday morning, the initial band of rainfall will\ndecrease in intensity and shift south and eastward over the \nrest of northern Minnesota. Temperatures on Tuesday will be \ncloser to normal as clouds and showers are ongoing. However, \nthe weather will become more active again by Tuesday evening as \nanother period of heavier rainfall enters northwest Wisconsin. \nThis secondary round of heavier rainfall is forecast to bring \nwidespread amounts ranging from a half to three quarters of an \ninch across northwest Wisconsin into Tuesday night. \n\nWednesday will feature one more warm day across the region, with \nlingering scattered rain showers mainly focusing on northwest \nWisconsin. These showers will gradually taper off as a potent \ncold front approaches from the west. This frontal passage \nWednesday night will usher in a pattern change for the \nNorthland. From the dawn hours Thursday into the daytime, expect\ngusty northwest winds to develop as a colder airmass filters \ninto the region. These brisk winds will accompany a sharp drop \nin temperatures, falling 10 to 15 degrees Thursday compared to \nthe warm conditions experienced on Wednesday.\n\nThe much colder airmass fully settles in for Thursday night\nbehind the cold front as strong surface high pressure sets up \noverhead. The combination of clearing skies, lightening winds, \nand cold air advection will create our next widespread frost and\nfreeze potential. Frost or freeze headlines are likely to be \nneeded in time for later Thursday night into Friday morning.\n\nNear-normal temperatures for early October will set up for this \nweekend as a frost potential will remain a concern during the \novernight hours. A minor and relatively weak weather system may \npass through the region by the weekend, bringing our next chance\nfor light rain showers to the area by Saturday night.",
